    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • The Volkswagen Group's Affordable BEV Quartet Has Almost Reached 150,000 Orders\n2027 Volkswagen T-Roc R Drops Camo at the Nurburgring, Looks Ready for Unveiling\n12 photos\nPhoto: SH Proshots\nTeased in November 2025, the upcoming T-Roc R is the whitest crossover at the Nordschleife right now. Caught lapping the German circuit in anger, the compact performance crossover wears little in the way of camouflage over its production-intent body panels. When the Volkswagen Group doesn't even try hiding a yet-unreleased model, that is when you know the debut is right around the corner.\nIts thin disguise covers a front end that appears unique to the R, distinct from the R Line that is all show and no extra go. For the series-production spec, Volkswagen is believed to use a lot of black to create the impression of a wider vehicle. A sizable lower intake can also be seen, along with a horizontal bar.\nEquipped with 20-inch lightweight wheels, the spied test vehicle is wearing 245-section rubber boots. Just as expected from an R, the brake calipers are painted in the performance sub-brand's trademark blue shade. While on the subject of brakes, the front and rear discs are larger than what you get on the R Line version.\nSlotting below the Tiguan, the T-Roc has forever been a pretty good-looking crossover. The R Line is even more, whereas the R brings the point home with a quad-piped Akrapovic titanium exhaust system. Its round outlets are housed within a specific valance panel with a diffuser-like central section. The gloss-black finish of the valance carriers over to the wheel arch cladding and side skirts.\nThe roof spoiler isn't all that different from the R Line. Actually, it sure looks as if Volkswagen saved a few euros by transplanting it altogether. This restraint fits the R sub-brand's habit of keeping its voice down. From certain angles, you can further see that Volkswagen's latest prototype is running a panoramic glass roof.\nPhoto: SH Proshots While pics of the interior have yet to be snapped, the 2027 Volkswagen T-Roc R is not going to impress anyone who experienced a 911 GT3. Expect only a bit more sportiness than you get in the R Line, nothing more. Building on lesser versions of the T-Roc , the R makes use of MQB Evo bones, albeit with a big twist.\nSaid twist concerns the rear axle, which integrates Magna electronically controlled multi-place clutch packs. One takes care of the left wheel, and the other works its magic on the right side. This design allows the electronic brain of the car to actively meter out torque mechanically to either side of the rear axle. Lesser 4MOTION, meanwhile, uses the brakes to slow down the spinning wheel.\nRegular all-wheel drive forces equal torque to both sides, while the Torque Splitter of the R directs up to 100 percent of the rear axle's torque to either wheel. This behavior overloads the outside rear wheel during hard cornering to pivot the vehicle, thus fighting understeer in a front-biased vehicle with a higher center of gravity than you get in the Golf R hatchback and its longroof-bodied sib.\nBecause of the MQB Evo connection to the Golf R twins, the T-Roc R crossover is certain to pack the very same iron-block turbo inline-four mill. In the compact hatchback and wagon, it delivers a mega 333 ps (328 hp) and 420 Nm (310 lb-ft) in European flavor, with maximum torque delivered at 2,100 revs.\nA manual isn't going to happen, not when Wolfsburg couldn't make a case for rowing your own in the Golf R. Alas, a dual-clutch box will have to suffice. If the German automaker borrows the DQ381 of the Golf R, then look forward to wet clutches instead of the DQ200's dry clutches. While the dry system is more efficient, its maximum torque capacity isn't good enough for this engine.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nVolkswagen T-Roc R 2027 Volkswagen T-Roc R Volkswagen spyshots design spy shots\nAbout the author: Mircea Panait\nAfter a 1:43 scale model of a Ferrari 250 GTO sparked Mircea's interest for cars when he was a kid, an early internship at Top Gear sealed his career path. He's most interested in muscle cars and American trucks, but he takes a passing interest in quirky kei cars as well. Full profile\nUP NEXT Nissan Sentra Gains Midnight Edition for 2027, Here's How Much It Costs North of the US Border",
